# Dependencies and Python versions

- Change dependencies with `uv`, not by editing `pyproject.toml` by
  hand: `uv add <pkg>` / `uv remove <pkg>` for what the code imports at
  run time, `uv add --group dev <pkg>` for what only tests, linting, or
  type checking need{% if is_library %}, and `uv add --optional <extra> <pkg>` for
  an optional extra{% endif %}. Commit `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock`
  together: CI's `lock` job runs `uv lock --check` and fails on a stale
  lock. The other jobs re-resolve against the CPU PyTorch index, so
  they cannot catch one.
- To move to a newer release, raise the minimum with
  `uv add "<pkg>>=X.Y.Z"` (keep the `--group`{% if is_library %} or `--optional`{% endif %} flag
  it was added with), or refresh only the lock with
  `uv lock --upgrade-package <pkg>`.
- `torch` and `torchvision` install from the `pytorch` index in
  `[[tool.uv.index]]`, which `[tool.uv.sources]` pins them to. Raising
  `torch`'s minimum also moves the README's PyTorch badge and its
  🖥️ Compute environment line; raise `torchvision` to the release
  paired with it, and check that the backend's index publishes both.
- Raising `ruff`'s minimum also raises `[tool.ruff] required-version`
  and the "omitted (ruff vX.Y.Z)" note at the end of `pyproject.toml`;
  re-check that the sections it lists still use only defaults.
- A runtime dependency's range is a requirement users see, so it goes
  in `CHANGELOG.md`; `dev` group changes stay out (see
  `.agents/changelog.md`).

## Supported Python versions

The supported versions are stated in several places. Change them
together, in one commit:

- `requires-python` in `pyproject.toml`: the oldest supported version;
- `[tool.ty.environment] python-version`: the same oldest version, so
  `ty` flags syntax and APIs newer than it;
- one `Programming Language :: Python :: X.Y` classifier per supported
  version;
- the `python-version` list in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`: every
  supported version, each tested on Linux;
- `.python-version`: the version development, and CI on Windows and
  macOS, run on (any supported one);
- the README's Python badge, which lists every supported version, and
  the requirement line in {% if is_library %}📦 Installation{% else %}📦 Setup{% endif %}.

The range comes from the template: the oldest version is the
`python_version` answer, and the newest is the newest version the
template offers. `copier update -d python_version=X.Y` moves the oldest
and re-renders all of the above and the *Project* line of
`AGENTS.md`; a plain `copier update` after the template offers a
newer Python extends the range. The newest version is also capped by
`torch`: a supported version needs `torch` and `torchvision` wheels on
the project's index and on the CPU index CI uses.

To leave a version out (a dependency has no wheels for it yet), drop
it from the classifiers, the CI list, and the badge together, and add
it back once the dependency catches up. Dropping a version a release
has supported is a `**Breaking:**` `Changed` entry in `CHANGELOG.md`;
supporting a new one is an `Added` entry.
